WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.166 --> 00:00:02.335
Hey, man, that
right there ain’t supposed to be like that.

00:00:02.377 --> 00:00:03.044
My elbows?

00:00:03.044 --> 00:00:03.920
That shouldn't be

00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:05.255
I’m double jointed.

00:00:05.547 --> 00:00:06.089
Yeah.

00:00:06.089 --> 00:00:06.881
That shouldn’t be like that.

00:00:06.881 --> 00:00:08.008
It helps swimming.

00:00:10.844 --> 00:00:11.594
Look like it hurt,

00:00:11.594 --> 00:00:12.095
didn't it?

00:00:15.098 --> 00:00:15.724
What, like,

00:00:15.724 --> 00:00:18.601
ultimately brought you to Phoenix?

00:00:18.727 --> 00:00:19.310
Like, what made you

00:00:19.310 --> 00:00:19.978
Bob

00:00:19.978 --> 00:00:20.603
My coach

00:00:20.603 --> 00:00:21.104
Really?

00:00:21.104 --> 00:00:21.396
Yeah.

00:00:21.730 --> 00:00:25.108
I mean, just because he,
so I was finishing my career.

00:00:25.108 --> 00:00:27.777
I had, like, two years, a year
and a half left in my career,

00:00:28.361 --> 00:00:32.365
and he goes, “I'm going to take the head
coaching job at ASU. Would you come?”

00:00:32.365 --> 00:00:34.117
and I was like, “Yeah, sure, why not?”

00:00:34.117 --> 00:00:38.621
For me, seeing the sunshine
every day is wonders.

00:00:38.621 --> 00:00:39.706
Just make you feel good.

00:00:39.706 --> 00:00:40.290
Yeah.

00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:42.584
Like you can wake up
and feel absolutely terrible

00:00:42.584 --> 00:00:45.587
and just go outside and stand in the sun
and get some nice vitamin D

00:00:45.587 --> 00:00:47.255
and you feel like a different person, so

00:00:48.465 --> 00:00:49.883
here we are five years later.

00:00:49.883 --> 00:00:51.009
The rest is history.

00:00:57.432 --> 00:00:58.516
Phelps, what’s happening?

00:00:59.100 --> 00:00:59.559
You alright?

00:00:59.559 --> 00:01:00.602
Yeah, can’t complain.

00:01:00.602 --> 00:01:02.228
Man, appreciate you being here.

00:01:02.812 --> 00:01:05.774
First and foremost, I thought we'd
do it here because we both love golf.

00:01:06.274 --> 00:01:07.692
We played golf a few times.

00:01:07.692 --> 00:01:09.694
I won’t talk about how bad I done beat him

00:01:09.694 --> 00:01:11.154
[laughing]

00:01:11.613 --> 00:01:13.198
but, you know,

00:01:13.198 --> 00:01:13.865
to start it off,

00:01:13.865 --> 00:01:18.161
just a little bit about yourself
and, you know, where you’re from, Baltimore.

00:01:18.745 --> 00:01:20.413
Born and raised in Baltimore, Maryland.

00:01:20.413 --> 00:01:21.998
The youngest of three.

00:01:21.998 --> 00:01:22.665
Baby boy.

00:01:22.665 --> 00:01:23.583
The only boy.

00:01:23.583 --> 00:01:26.169
I played baseball, lacrosse, soccer,
and swam

00:01:26.419 --> 00:01:30.423
till I was 11 years old
and then decided to focus on swimming.

00:01:30.423 --> 00:01:32.926
Only because my coach told me
I could make the Olympic team.

00:01:32.926 --> 00:01:35.261
And then everything kind of took off.

00:01:35.261 --> 00:01:38.264
But did somebody swim, like, around
you at like, I know your mom?

00:01:38.348 --> 00:01:38.640
Yeah.

00:01:38.765 --> 00:01:41.935
So, we, so my sisters both swam.

00:01:42.519 --> 00:01:45.313
Whitney,
my middle sister, was internationally,

00:01:45.313 --> 00:01:48.733
what? Number three in the world?
Number one in the nation at 15.

00:01:48.733 --> 00:01:49.567
Or 13, excuse me.

00:01:49.567 --> 00:01:50.110
Swimming?

00:01:50.110 --> 00:01:50.485
Swimming.

00:01:51.277 --> 00:01:52.153
I never knew that.

00:01:52.153 --> 00:01:55.240
And then I
saw her just doing all these cool things

00:01:55.240 --> 00:01:56.282
and I was like, I want to do that.

00:01:56.282 --> 00:01:59.410
Like, I want to go to Rome and swim
there and go to world championships

00:01:59.410 --> 00:02:00.745
and have a chance to go to the Olympics.

00:02:00.745 --> 00:02:05.291
So at the age of 11, my coach sat down
with myself, my parents, and was like,

00:02:05.667 --> 00:02:08.586
“You have an opportunity to do
something really special in four years”

00:02:09.003 --> 00:02:10.672
“if.” And I was like, “Okay, what?”

00:02:10.672 --> 00:02:11.506
If what?

00:02:12.048 --> 00:02:14.300
No other sports, you're
dropping the other sports.

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:16.594
So I was focused on swimming
and that was it.

00:02:17.053 --> 00:02:22.058
So as an 11 year old kid, I heard the word
Olympics and I was all in.

00:02:22.058 --> 00:02:22.725
That was it.

00:02:23.143 --> 00:02:24.853
That young, at 11?

00:02:25.103 --> 00:02:25.311
Yeah.

00:02:25.311 --> 00:02:27.897
Just having the opportunity
to represent your country,

00:02:27.897 --> 00:02:30.817
win an Olympic gold medal. Like for me,
I wanted to break a world record, and

00:02:31.276 --> 00:02:34.612
the only route to be able to have that
chance was swimming at the highest level.

00:02:34.737 --> 00:02:36.072
What was it like then, though?

00:02:36.072 --> 00:02:39.117
Like, what was the preparation
like at 11

00:02:39.117 --> 00:02:42.328
for like swimming? Was you like swimming
before school, swimming after school?

00:02:43.413 --> 00:02:44.330
I mean, back then I was

00:02:44.330 --> 00:02:47.000
probably only swimming a handful of times
a week, maybe five days a week.

00:02:47.125 --> 00:02:48.877
But you was always that nice?

00:02:48.877 --> 00:02:51.129
Uhhh, probably not.

00:02:51.129 --> 00:02:53.381
I think I got better
and better pretty quick.

00:02:53.715 --> 00:02:56.593
So when you was in high school, right, like,

00:02:56.593 --> 00:02:59.888
I was never caught near a pool, right?

00:02:59.888 --> 00:03:04.058
Like I taught myself how to swim on a AAU trip
just because I was scared.

00:03:04.058 --> 00:03:04.726
I was scared.

00:03:04.726 --> 00:03:06.936
You would get in the pool,

00:03:07.604 --> 00:03:08.980
everybody would be playing around.

00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:12.859
And, you know, especially
in Black households, somebody even thought

00:03:12.859 --> 00:03:14.986
about pushing you in the pool
it was going to start a fight.

00:03:14.986 --> 00:03:15.445
[laughing]

00:03:15.445 --> 00:03:16.654
It was going to start a fight.

00:03:16.654 --> 00:03:23.286
So when you were in high school and stuff,
were your meets, like, not even close?

00:03:23.494 --> 00:03:26.206
Like, when did you start
knowing, like, “Okay, I'm”

00:03:26.372 --> 00:03:27.040
“I'm nice”?

00:03:28.416 --> 00:03:30.501
I was breaking national age group records

00:03:30.501 --> 00:03:34.005
from the age of eight on, like,

00:03:34.214 --> 00:03:38.426
I mean, I probably broke 200 national age
group records. So national age group

00:03:38.468 --> 00:03:41.471
records are the fastest time swam
in that age group.

00:03:42.013 --> 00:03:45.934
So I was 15, 16 breaking the open records.

00:03:46.601 --> 00:03:49.604
So as a 16 year old or 15 year old,
I made my first Olympic team.

00:03:50.396 --> 00:03:52.232
Um, yeah, that's different.

00:03:52.232 --> 00:03:53.191
That's very different.

00:03:53.191 --> 00:03:56.110
And so let's talk for a second
about that type of spotlight.

00:03:56.444 --> 00:03:58.780
Like, what was that like?

00:04:00.490 --> 00:04:02.408
I was a deer in headlights
in my first Olympics,

00:04:02.408 --> 00:04:05.245
a 15 year old kid,
never traveled outside of the country.

00:04:05.453 --> 00:04:07.747
I'm here standing an Olympic final

00:04:07.747 --> 00:04:10.917
All I wanted to do was get one second 
faster than I did at trials.

00:04:11.501 --> 00:04:14.796
I got 90, 98, 96/100ths faster

00:04:15.129 --> 00:04:18.007
and I was less than three tenths

00:04:18.007 --> 00:04:21.135
out of a second from a medal,
and I wanted a medal.

00:04:21.803 --> 00:04:27.225
And that motivation from,
from not medaling catapulted me into 2004.

00:04:29.227 --> 00:04:29.686
I say

00:04:29.686 --> 00:04:32.939
I fall short
in 2004 because I wasn't perfect, right?

00:04:32.981 --> 00:04:34.148
I lost.

00:04:34.315 --> 00:04:35.858
I don't like to lose.
We don't like to lose.

00:04:35.858 --> 00:04:36.276
So

00:04:36.276 --> 00:04:37.193
Yeah, exactly.

00:04:37.193 --> 00:04:42.156
So I use that motivation to then
catapult me into 2008 to where

00:04:43.116 --> 00:04:46.035
we had all the tools that we needed
at that point

00:04:46.035 --> 00:04:49.747
to be able to compete at that level
at every single race.

00:04:50.290 --> 00:04:54.002
During that week in Beijing
I think I swam 60,000 meters.

00:04:54.252 --> 00:04:57.964
So during a regular training week
I was swimming 80 to 100,000 meters.

00:04:58.506 --> 00:05:02.385
So because of all the warm ups,
warm downs, this, that, and the other,

00:05:02.593 --> 00:05:04.262
I had to be extremely prepared.

00:05:04.262 --> 00:05:08.975
And that week took eight years
to get prepared for. I had to swim

00:05:09.267 --> 00:05:13.021
that event program
over and over and over again.

00:05:13.187 --> 00:05:15.606
So in '08, I didn't see anything else.

00:05:15.606 --> 00:05:18.443
I was like, almost like a racehorse

00:05:18.443 --> 00:05:19.235
with blinders on.

00:05:19.235 --> 00:05:19.777
 Right.

00:05:19.777 --> 00:05:21.821
You was just so locked in
                 —I knew exactly what I had to do.

00:05:21.821 --> 00:05:26.617
I knew like to the second, I knew where
I had to be at every, every single day.

00:05:26.743 --> 00:05:28.536
What was the crazy thing

00:05:28.536 --> 00:05:31.247
everybody used to talk about, how
many calories or something you used to have to eat?

00:05:31.247 --> 00:05:31.956
What was it?

00:05:31.956 --> 00:05:36.044
So I was eating eight to 10,000 calories
every day because when we're swimming,

00:05:36.336 --> 00:05:38.671
I can fluctuate
between five and ten pounds in a week.

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:42.884
And when we're trying to be at our best,
we want to be at race weight at all times.

00:05:43.259 --> 00:05:45.303
So I'm forcing myself to eat.

00:05:45.303 --> 00:05:48.431
I don't like to eat now.
Like, I can't stand to eat.

00:05:48.431 --> 00:05:50.933
I eat what I need to, to

00:05:51.225 --> 00:05:52.769
When you say eight
                    —give my body what it needs.

00:05:52.769 --> 00:05:55.104
You said eight to 10,000 calories a day.

00:05:55.104 --> 00:05:57.190
What's that look like?

00:05:57.190 --> 00:05:58.274
Whatever.

00:05:58.274 --> 00:06:02.528
Literally whatever, because you can't eat
all good foods and get that many calories.

00:06:02.528 --> 00:06:02.862
 Right.

00:06:02.862 --> 00:06:05.948
So I'm mixing a pint of ice cream

00:06:05.948 --> 00:06:10.745
or milkshakes or cookies or like,
I mean a breakfast would have been,

00:06:11.412 --> 00:06:13.915
you know,
two breakfast sandwiches, a bowl of grits,

00:06:14.123 --> 00:06:17.502
an omelet, short
stack of chocolate chip pancakes.

00:06:17.502 --> 00:06:21.756
And then I'd go take a nap
and then I'd wake up, have,

00:06:21.756 --> 00:06:24.926
I don't know, a bagel
and a PowerBar or a bagel and some fruit.

00:06:24.967 --> 00:06:26.761
–If I do that, I’m gonna blow up
            –swim for another two hours.

00:06:26.761 --> 00:06:28.388
If I do that, I'm gonna blow up.

00:06:28.388 --> 00:06:30.181
I'm gonna blow up. That is out.

00:06:30.181 --> 00:06:31.766
I cannot do that.
—But

00:06:31.766 --> 00:06:32.850
I mean, I had to maintain that weight.

00:06:32.850 --> 00:06:37.063
So I mean, but and the crazy thing
now, like, I think back to it,

00:06:37.146 --> 00:06:38.648
I think the reason why I don't like to eat

00:06:38.648 --> 00:06:40.858
is because there are always people over
my shoulder being like,

00:06:41.025 --> 00:06:42.068
“You're not eating enough.”

00:06:42.068 --> 00:06:43.736
Like, “You got to put food into your system,”

00:06:43.736 --> 00:06:45.988
you know, 30 minutes
after a workout, right? Like,

00:06:45.988 --> 00:06:47.740
–You’re a machine
          –Yes

00:06:47.740 --> 00:06:49.409
And that's what it, that's what it felt like.

00:06:49.409 --> 00:06:51.327
So for me, I'm like, no, I just

00:06:51.327 --> 00:06:53.496
I want to eat when I want to eat,
what I want to eat.

00:06:54.664 --> 00:06:57.500
If I say this name to you, Bob Bowman,
right?

00:06:57.500 --> 00:07:02.713
That’s a coach that you're still close with.
In what I've done over the years,

00:07:02.713 --> 00:07:05.675
whether it be football or basketball,
I've had a ton of different coaches.

00:07:05.675 --> 00:07:09.137
What is it like to have that relationship
with someone for a long time?

00:07:09.345 --> 00:07:11.597
He’s a dad, like,

00:07:11.597 --> 00:07:13.433
I didn't
I didn't always have a dad growing up.

00:07:13.433 --> 00:07:16.352
So for me, having somebody there that was

00:07:17.728 --> 00:07:19.188
one: believed in you

00:07:19.188 --> 00:07:22.150
had confidence in you,
but also helped you along the way.

00:07:23.025 --> 00:07:25.069
I learned a lot from him
and I still learn a lot from him.

00:07:25.236 --> 00:07:27.697
He's somebody that comes to the house
almost every day.

00:07:27.947 --> 00:07:29.240
He's grandpa to the boys. 

00:07:29.240 --> 00:07:29.699
Still?

00:07:29.699 --> 00:07:30.658
Still
       –Yep

00:07:30.658 --> 00:07:35.580
So he's coaching here at ASU
and yeah, he's in the pool

00:07:35.580 --> 00:07:39.459
with the boys when the weather's nice
and he’s just grandpa.

00:07:39.917 --> 00:07:42.545
There were many times
when you contemplated

00:07:42.545 --> 00:07:45.173
like retiring, retiring.

00:07:45.173 --> 00:07:45.882
Why didn't you?

00:07:45.882 --> 00:07:46.716
You ain't got–

00:07:46.716 --> 00:07:49.093
not just because me and Frank told
you not to,

00:07:49.093 --> 00:07:50.887
but, but why?

00:07:50.887 --> 00:07:51.679
Why didn't you?

00:07:51.679 --> 00:07:55.016
The fuel that was inside of me.
I mean, for me,

00:07:55.016 --> 00:07:57.477
like, in 2012 when I retired,

00:07:59.187 --> 00:08:00.480
it was, it was,

00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:04.692
it was more of a break than anything else,
you know, because for so long

00:08:04.859 --> 00:08:08.946
my head was staring at a black line
and I couldn't do anything else.

00:08:08.946 --> 00:08:10.198
I was never a kid.

00:08:10.198 --> 00:08:12.450
And so I wanted to find out
what being a kid was like

00:08:12.617 --> 00:08:17.121
So I think at that point, for me
to have that kind of growing up process

00:08:17.121 --> 00:08:21.542
and seeing a little bit on my own,
learning a little bit on my own, like,

00:08:21.584 --> 00:08:24.253
I just couldn't walk away.
I didn't want to walk away.

00:08:24.253 --> 00:08:27.048
But the time was right
for me to walk away when I did. And

00:08:28.132 --> 00:08:29.759
that didn't
answer your question at all, but

00:08:29.759 --> 00:08:31.135
Oh, it did.

00:08:31.135 --> 00:08:32.428
I'm over here just

00:08:32.428 --> 00:08:36.432
thinking about how the fact that I got,
I got two Olympic gold medals

00:08:36.432 --> 00:08:39.060
and I thought I had done,
I'd done something, right?

00:08:39.477 --> 00:08:43.314
You sitting over there with 23 gold medals.
Like which one

00:08:43.314 --> 00:08:46.526
is sort of like the most important,
is the one where you won by a point?

00:08:46.567 --> 00:08:46.984
Yeah.

00:08:46.984 --> 00:08:50.196
I mean, honestly, from a textbook

00:08:50.571 --> 00:08:53.449
like best performance,

00:08:53.866 --> 00:08:56.577
I would say
it's probably the 200 free from Beijing.

00:08:57.995 --> 00:09:01.707
Yeah and I think that my favorite
besides that has to be the four-peat.

00:09:02.041 --> 00:09:04.627
To win the same event
over four straight Olympics.

00:09:05.211 --> 00:09:07.421
Like that's kind of crazy to think about.

00:09:07.421 --> 00:09:12.760
I think I could see it in your face too,
like in '16, right, because

00:09:13.719 --> 00:09:15.346
you sort of the old guy

00:09:15.471 --> 00:09:17.139
You know what I mean?
                   –I was the dad of the team.

00:09:17.139 --> 00:09:18.599
Right, right. Basically.

00:09:18.599 --> 00:09:23.980
And I know you, how competitive you are,
and so probably one of the best feelings is

00:09:23.980 --> 00:09:27.400
doing what everybody thinks that you can't, right?

00:09:28.067 --> 00:09:29.694
How was that?

00:09:29.694 --> 00:09:33.447
I mean, I have–my whole career was filled
with people who doubted me.

00:09:33.948 --> 00:09:36.659
Um, you know, going into 2008

00:09:36.659 --> 00:09:38.744
Thorpe said I'd never win
eight gold medals, but

00:09:39.912 --> 00:09:41.539
Thank you, Ian

00:09:41.539 --> 00:09:44.375
And he knows, he knows
that was, I think he said

00:09:44.375 --> 00:09:47.336
afterwards, he goes, “I know how you work
and I know how to motivate you”

00:09:48.129 --> 00:09:50.381
To go from that spotlight year in

00:09:50.381 --> 00:09:53.301
and year out to retirement,

00:09:53.301 --> 00:09:54.885
what was that like?

00:09:57.305 --> 00:09:58.139
Um,

00:09:58.139 --> 00:09:59.098
it's weird

00:09:59.765 --> 00:10:01.434
Going from

00:10:01.434 --> 00:10:06.230
basically every single day
is scheduled, perfect–

00:10:06.230 --> 00:10:08.316
Like, you don't have to think, right?

00:10:08.316 --> 00:10:11.235
You get up, you go to practice, you do this,
you eat, you do this, you fall asleep 

00:10:11.235 --> 00:10:14.488
you go back to practice, you ice tub, massage,

00:10:14.530 --> 00:10:16.282
I still can’t imagine
               –eat, go back to sleep, like

00:10:16.282 --> 00:10:16.949
all this stuff

00:10:17.533 --> 00:10:19.785
I mean, I did it all for 20 years
straight.

00:10:20.828 --> 00:10:22.955
So like, you take that away,

00:10:23.998 --> 00:10:26.167
I feel like a kid.
I don't know what I'm doing.

00:10:26.834 --> 00:10:30.338
So for me, like having three kids now,

00:10:30.546 --> 00:10:33.007
I've learned as they've learned,

00:10:34.175 --> 00:10:35.968
because my childhood was swimming. 

00:10:35.968 --> 00:10:36.427
Mm-hmm.

00:10:36.636 --> 00:10:37.845
And that was it.

00:10:37.970 --> 00:10:40.264
So I'm learning through this process of

00:10:40.765 --> 00:10:43.476
trying to fulfill my day

00:10:43.476 --> 00:10:47.229
with things that make me, me
and make me the best me.

00:10:47.396 --> 00:10:48.064
 Right.

00:10:48.272 --> 00:10:50.816
You showed a lot of courage
to speak about mental health

00:10:50.816 --> 00:10:54.320
and talk about the things
that you've dealt with in life.

00:10:54.695 --> 00:10:58.115
What finally gave you
that courage and wanted you to

00:10:59.408 --> 00:11:01.952
talk about it
and share it? Because we all deal with it.

00:11:01.952 --> 00:11:05.498
I think it's just a lot of times,
you know, people don't talk about it.

00:11:05.706 --> 00:11:06.290
It's crazy.

00:11:06.290 --> 00:11:08.918
I go back and

00:11:08.918 --> 00:11:10.711
it was 2014.

00:11:10.711 --> 00:11:14.965
Sports Illustrated cover story
with Tim Layden and 

00:11:14.965 --> 00:11:19.387
we're having an interview here
in Phoenix and

00:11:19.387 --> 00:11:22.973
whatever question he asked me

00:11:22.973 --> 00:11:24.850
just opened me up,

00:11:24.892 --> 00:11:27.103
dropped my shoulders
and I just let it all out

00:11:29.021 --> 00:11:32.566
I was just ready to, you know.
I think for me when I checked myself

00:11:32.566 --> 00:11:35.319
in to get treatment and to get help,

00:11:36.112 --> 00:11:38.656
I knew that that's what I needed

00:11:38.656 --> 00:11:41.701
and I was at the end of the road
and I had to get help.

00:11:42.159 --> 00:11:44.912
I was at a scary place and time,

00:11:44.912 --> 00:11:46.956
and for a long time,
I didn't know how to ask for help.

00:11:47.873 --> 00:11:51.627
I think once I asked help for the first
time, everything became easy.

00:11:51.627 --> 00:11:56.716
So that question, when he asked me
or whatever it was, it just opened me up

00:11:56.716 --> 00:11:59.260
and I felt comfortable
to share everything that I'd been through

00:11:59.885 --> 00:12:03.222
because it was almost like I said
to myself, “I don't care what people think.”

00:12:03.848 --> 00:12:07.685
“This is me and this is my journey
and it makes me who I am.”

00:12:07.685 --> 00:12:11.981
So you know, the only way
for me to learn and grow and to get over

00:12:11.981 --> 00:12:15.651
certain things that I've gone through
is to communicate and to talk about it.

00:12:15.651 --> 00:12:17.903
And look, I'm not perfect, right?

00:12:17.945 --> 00:12:18.946
Like, nobody's perfect.

00:12:18.946 --> 00:12:21.615
And I struggled a lot through my life.

00:12:21.907 --> 00:12:23.826
My first depression spell
was in 2004,

00:12:25.244 --> 00:12:28.414
and I went up and down rollercoasters
all throughout life.

00:12:28.497 --> 00:12:29.373
Like we all do.

00:12:29.373 --> 00:12:33.878
And I think the coolest part about it is
not that you do it for that reason at all,

00:12:33.878 --> 00:12:38.257
because you're doing it for yourself,
but you just speaking about it.

00:12:38.382 --> 00:12:42.011
It just gave so many other people
a lot more courage, not just athletes,

00:12:42.011 --> 00:12:43.137
just people in general.

00:12:43.137 --> 00:12:46.432
Why you think so many people
are having a hard time admitting

00:12:46.432 --> 00:12:50.603
to grappling
with, like, these mental issues and...?

00:12:51.729 --> 00:12:53.522
I think it's vulnerability.

00:12:53.522 --> 00:12:55.816
I think vulnerability is a scary word
for a lot of people,

00:12:56.025 --> 00:12:58.194
and it's a scary word for me.

00:12:58.194 --> 00:13:00.946
I remember when I checked
into a treatment center, I was shaking

00:13:01.405 --> 00:13:04.241
when I went in there because I didn't know
what I'm getting myself into, but

00:13:05.034 --> 00:13:06.285
I wanted help.

00:13:06.285 --> 00:13:08.204
I didn't like feeling the way that I did.

00:13:08.204 --> 00:13:10.247
So what else is out there?

00:13:10.247 --> 00:13:11.332
I started asking questions.

00:13:11.332 --> 00:13:15.169
And look, you know, for me
as a male athlete, I thought like, I–

00:13:15.586 --> 00:13:16.962
I couldn't talk about it. 

00:13:16.962 --> 00:13:18.088
Right, like you almost–

00:13:18.255 --> 00:13:19.715
You're giving away weaknesses
–weakness, right

00:13:19.715 --> 00:13:22.343
Yeah, like you're giving away
something to your competitors.

00:13:22.343 --> 00:13:26.555
And, you know, that's hard to do
when you're in this field of battle.

00:13:26.555 --> 00:13:31.727
So, you know, my approach now is if we're
physically strong and mentally strong.

00:13:32.269 --> 00:13:33.479
Oh, wow.

00:13:33.562 --> 00:13:34.855
You put those things together.

00:13:34.855 --> 00:13:37.191
–Yeah. 
–We’re unstoppable.
–Unstoppable.

00:13:37.191 --> 00:13:40.611
So you now live here in Phoenix,
this beautiful weather,

00:13:41.695 --> 00:13:43.697
your amazing wife, Nicole.

00:13:43.697 --> 00:13:46.033
You have three beautiful kids, all boys.

00:13:46.242 --> 00:13:49.662
You once said that “she's seen the good,
the bad, and the ugly.”

00:13:50.079 --> 00:13:51.455
What do you mean by that?

00:13:51.455 --> 00:13:57.044
Yeah, you know,
I mean, she's seen me as, you know,

00:13:57.753 --> 00:13:59.839
an Olympic champion.

00:14:00.798 --> 00:14:02.925
Me as a dad,

00:14:02.925 --> 00:14:07.346
me depressed, me feeling like 
the worst person on the planet.

00:14:07.388 --> 00:14:08.472
Like, she's seen all of it.

00:14:08.472 --> 00:14:10.808
She's seen every single side of me.

00:14:10.808 --> 00:14:14.144
And look, I can sit here and talk about it
because it's who I am,

00:14:14.144 --> 00:14:15.563
and I love who I am.

00:14:15.563 --> 00:14:17.982
It's taken a long time for me
to look in the mirror and like who I am.

00:14:18.315 --> 00:14:19.984
So for me, like,

00:14:19.984 --> 00:14:22.987
I'll say the hardest thing was going
through the pandemic.

00:14:22.987 --> 00:14:28.117
Being able to understand and manage my emotions
through the pandemic was overwhelming.

00:14:28.242 --> 00:14:28.742
 Right.

00:14:28.742 --> 00:14:33.455
Like, some of the scariest moments
of my life, and without my wife there,

00:14:33.873 --> 00:14:35.124
I don't know if I'd be here today.

00:14:36.166 --> 00:14:39.461
Yeah, the Covid pandemic has been called
a mental health crisis.

00:14:39.461 --> 00:14:41.505
Obviously I went to the bubble.

00:14:41.505 --> 00:14:44.383
People are still dealing with it,
especially kids.

00:14:44.383 --> 00:14:45.551
Especially children, right?

00:14:45.551 --> 00:14:49.388
Like, kids being at home school,
doing everything on a computer and Zoom.

00:14:49.638 --> 00:14:52.474
What’s something that you think can help
these kids

00:14:52.474 --> 00:14:55.185
that are facing these
different challenges?

00:14:55.686 --> 00:15:00.566
Loneliness has struck us
more than anything, in my opinion,

00:15:00.941 --> 00:15:02.318
over the last two years.

00:15:02.318 --> 00:15:05.988
And loneliness leads to depression,
which leads to other things.

00:15:06.071 --> 00:15:11.493
So for me it's trying to do whatever you can
to have normalcy in your life.

00:15:11.493 --> 00:15:13.037
Like, what does that look like?

00:15:13.037 --> 00:15:16.206
You know, like, I have no idea. Like,

00:15:16.206 --> 00:15:17.374
you know what you do, right?

00:15:17.374 --> 00:15:19.335
Like, for me, I have three kids,

00:15:19.335 --> 00:15:20.544
two of them are at school.

00:15:20.544 --> 00:15:23.380
I mean, I play golf. I sit at home.

00:15:23.380 --> 00:15:24.465
–Trust me, I know
–I work from home.

00:15:24.465 --> 00:15:27.092
I don't travel anymore. Like, you know?

00:15:27.092 --> 00:15:29.428
I live here, 
my family is in L.A., so I get it.

00:15:29.553 --> 00:15:32.723
It's hard, but it's like,
I think trying to find a routine,

00:15:33.015 --> 00:15:34.975
some kind of routine,
I think is always important.

00:15:34.975 --> 00:15:39.229
And as athletes, it's something that we know
and we do every single day of our life,

00:15:39.605 --> 00:15:41.774
but finding
some kind of normal routine,

00:15:41.774 --> 00:15:43.817
because then you get to feel like you.

00:15:43.817 --> 00:15:47.655
Because the moment you start feeling
not yourself, you start spiraling.

00:15:47.655 --> 00:15:49.823
So I think finding ways to be
your authentic self

00:15:49.823 --> 00:15:52.451
is something that's so important.
So self-care, right?

00:15:52.785 --> 00:15:54.620
I work out every day,
six, seven days a week.

00:15:54.620 --> 00:15:55.871
That's something I have to do for me.

00:15:55.871 --> 00:15:59.291
I go and hit golf balls once or twice
a week, put headsets in, and I just hit

00:15:59.291 --> 00:16:02.670
golf balls like I'm in my own world
and sometimes I go swim.

00:16:02.962 --> 00:16:04.838
But I need that

00:16:04.838 --> 00:16:07.967
in order to be the best dad that I can,
to be the best husband that I can.

00:16:08.509 --> 00:16:11.220
So, you know,
we want to give ourselves a chance.

00:16:11.261 --> 00:16:12.846
So how can we give ourselves
a chance?

00:16:12.846 --> 00:16:14.098
Yeah, let’s, uh–

00:16:14.473 --> 00:16:17.476
Let’s hit this range and see what the, see
what that swing looking like these days.

00:16:17.476 --> 00:16:17.977
I like it.

00:16:17.977 --> 00:16:18.435
Yeah.

00:16:23.649 --> 00:16:25.693
You know, something I always wanted 
to ask you, which is,

00:16:27.194 --> 00:16:30.906
I mean, you're used to it, but it seemed like
it would be kind of weird to me, right?

00:16:30.906 --> 00:16:33.450
So in some of your meets or races,

00:16:35.202 --> 00:16:37.496
you have teammates, right?
–Yeah.

00:16:37.579 --> 00:16:38.747
Like, you have teammates.

00:16:38.747 --> 00:16:41.417
But then you may do a meet where

00:16:41.583 --> 00:16:44.503
the person who was just your teammate
–Mm-hmm

00:16:44.503 --> 00:16:46.630
–You going against them. 
–Yeah.

00:16:46.630 --> 00:16:47.798
–I mean, we know
–Is that weird?

00:16:47.798 --> 00:16:48.674
No, not really.

00:16:48.674 --> 00:16:49.425
Because

00:16:50.426 --> 00:16:52.386
you know, most of the time,
like internationally,

00:16:52.386 --> 00:16:54.138
we're wearing Stars and Stripes, right?

00:16:54.138 --> 00:16:55.389
So there's only two of us.

00:16:55.389 --> 00:16:58.225
So the chance of us going head to head
is pretty likely.

00:16:58.434 --> 00:16:59.643
Yeah, but still

00:16:59.643 --> 00:17:00.602
It’s weird,

00:17:00.644 --> 00:17:03.188
but no, I mean, I think we can,
we can kind of put it past us.

00:17:03.188 --> 00:17:07.109
And we know when we step up
on the block, it's business.

00:17:07.109 --> 00:17:09.653
It's competition, like,
it’s not like we don't like you,

00:17:09.653 --> 00:17:10.696
we just want to beat the hell out of you.

00:17:11.947 --> 00:17:12.865
What about the kids, though?

00:17:12.865 --> 00:17:14.908
How are they with the water?

00:17:14.908 --> 00:17:15.659
They love it.

00:17:16.076 --> 00:17:17.745
They all absolutely love it.

00:17:17.745 --> 00:17:21.832
Boomer is, Boomer swims
butterfly and freestyle.

00:17:21.832 --> 00:17:24.793
Beckett goes underwater.
And he's like our little dolphin.

00:17:24.960 --> 00:17:26.462
Boomer's five now, right? 
–Yeah.

00:17:26.462 --> 00:17:28.922
I can't wait till he...

00:17:30.758 --> 00:17:32.342
I'm trying to think, like,

00:17:32.342 --> 00:17:35.345
when will Boomer realize,

00:17:36.430 --> 00:17:37.181
like,

00:17:39.058 --> 00:17:39.767
who you are?

00:17:39.767 --> 00:17:40.350
You know what I mean?

00:17:40.350 --> 00:17:42.895
Like, I'm sure he knows

00:17:42.895 --> 00:17:46.106
about the water or whatnot,
but he doesn't really know yet, right?

00:17:46.148 --> 00:17:47.566
He calls me Aquaman.

00:17:47.566 --> 00:17:50.778
Like, they all call me Aquaman,

00:17:51.236 --> 00:17:52.071
but no, I don't,

00:17:52.071 --> 00:17:54.281
I don't think he fully gets it yet. And he,

00:17:55.657 --> 00:17:58.327
he'll always say, he's like,
“Can you beat that person?”

00:17:58.327 --> 00:17:59.620
“Can you beat that person?” 
I'll be like, 

00:17:59.620 --> 00:18:01.663
“Look, back in the day, yes.
Not anymore, though.”

00:18:17.471 --> 00:18:19.848
Man, this looks nice.

00:18:19.848 --> 00:18:22.267
Feels so much better in the sun.
–Oh my gosh.

00:18:35.072 --> 00:18:38.700
Good roll.

00:18:38.700 --> 00:18:39.618
Dang,

00:18:39.618 --> 00:18:41.578
you must live here or something.

00:18:41.578 --> 00:18:42.663
Shhhh

00:18:43.330 --> 00:18:45.040
A question that I always

00:18:45.040 --> 00:18:47.084
wonder is if you didn't swim,
what would you do?

00:18:48.210 --> 00:18:50.796
If I could choose anything,
I would want to be a golfer

00:18:51.004 --> 00:18:52.714
just because I'm obsessed with the game.

00:18:52.714 --> 00:18:54.383
We do that all the time.

00:18:54.383 --> 00:18:57.719
But in reality, if I had to choose a sport

00:18:57.719 --> 00:19:01.598
that I was good at,
I was a decent, decent baseball player.

00:19:01.598 --> 00:19:03.225
Growing up, I played catcher.

00:19:03.225 --> 00:19:06.061
I was always throwing the mask off,
so I was breaking the mask all the time.

00:19:06.061 --> 00:19:07.771
Baseball players get paid.

00:19:07.771 --> 00:19:09.148
They do, uh

00:19:09.481 --> 00:19:10.274
Salary cap

00:19:10.274 --> 00:19:11.233
Or a lacrosse player.

00:19:11.233 --> 00:19:12.693
That's the other thing.
–Lacrosse?

00:19:12.693 --> 00:19:14.486
I grew up on the East Coast,
so I played lacrosse

00:19:14.486 --> 00:19:15.487
Oh, man.

00:19:15.654 --> 00:19:17.865
So, yeah, I was a long stick middie

00:19:18.323 --> 00:19:21.160
and, um, and then when I was 11,

00:19:21.160 --> 00:19:24.413
my coach just basically said,
no more and focus on swimming

00:19:25.664 --> 00:19:27.916
So, um,

00:19:27.916 --> 00:19:29.334
what's next?

00:19:31.211 --> 00:19:34.506
Besides me hopefully making this putt?

00:19:34.673 --> 00:19:36.842
I mean, my biggest thing now is

00:19:37.634 --> 00:19:40.512
all really around mental health.

00:19:41.638 --> 00:19:42.139
I want to,

00:19:42.139 --> 00:19:45.309
I want to try and lower the suicide number
as much as I can.

00:19:45.309 --> 00:19:47.394
There are too many people
that are taking their own lives,

00:19:47.895 --> 00:19:50.606
and I want to help
the people that are out there suffering.

00:19:50.606 --> 00:19:54.234
When you get a chance to hear somebody say
that you changed and impact their lives,

00:19:54.234 --> 00:19:57.613
that's, that's probably even more gratifying

00:19:57.613 --> 00:19:58.947
than a gold medal now.

00:19:58.947 --> 00:20:01.366
It's the best feeling in the world.

00:20:01.491 --> 00:20:03.952
For me, being able to have

00:20:05.621 --> 00:20:07.539
people share their experiences,

00:20:07.539 --> 00:20:10.751
their moments, what they've been through,

00:20:10.751 --> 00:20:12.377
showing their vulnerability.

00:20:12.377 --> 00:20:15.631
Oh, my gosh, dude.
It, you can't put it into words.

00:20:15.631 --> 00:20:16.506
It's the greatest feeling.

00:20:18.300 --> 00:20:21.637
The one thing I can say is
I encourage every single person out there

00:20:22.179 --> 00:20:27.935
to communicate, to open up,
to talk, don't stuff things down, don't

00:20:27.935 --> 00:20:32.356
compartmentalize, because that stuff just
piles up, piles up on top of each other.

00:20:32.773 --> 00:20:35.817
And before you know it,
it all just explodes.

00:20:36.401 --> 00:20:37.027
That’s real.

00:20:37.027 --> 00:20:39.029
I say that from firsthand because I know,

00:20:39.029 --> 00:20:41.448
I was really good at compartmentalizing
throughout my career,

00:20:42.491 --> 00:20:44.826
and I can't do that anymore.

00:20:44.993 --> 00:20:46.245
That's real.

00:20:47.454 --> 00:20:48.372
Well.

00:20:48.789 --> 00:20:49.665
Oh, man.

00:20:49.665 --> 00:20:50.499
Appreciate you.

00:20:50.499 --> 00:20:50.832
For sure.

00:20:50.832 --> 00:20:51.458
For real.

00:20:51.458 --> 00:20:53.460
Always. Definitely. 

00:20:53.460 --> 00:20:54.586
Thank you.

